; ------------------------------------------------------------------ weapons
; What is in the player's hands, and nothing else. Exactly one owner, so the
; sword and the chain cannot both believe the same click was theirs.
[node name="WeaponSlot" type="Node" parent="."]
script = ExtResource("11")

; The hand that changes them. `1` sword / `2` phantom chain, the unequip cleanup
; that stops a mid-air chain and closes a mid-swing hitbox, and the dev weapon
; indicator. It is the slot's only runtime writer and it owns no weapon logic.
[node name="WeaponManager" type="Node" parent="."]
script = ExtResource("13")

; 缚星链. Star-Bind Chain — still present for labs/racks; not on hotkey 2.
; Hotkey 2 is Phantom Chain (虚链) below.
[node name="ChainDirector" type="Node3D" parent="."]
script = ExtResource("12")

; 虚链 / PHANTOM CHAIN — independent rule-based combat workstream. Key 2.
; Do not fold this into sword_controller or ChainDirector.
[node name="PhantomChainController" type="Node3D" parent="."]
script = ExtResource("14")

; 镜 / MIRROR — independent position/angle/travel workstream. Key 3.
; Do not fold this into sword, ChainDirector, or PhantomChainController.
[node name="MirrorWeaponController" type="Node3D" parent="."]
script = ExtResource("15")

; The first-person hand. The handle rides the camera and the chain runs from here
; out into the world, which is the only way a first-person chain reads as
; attached to the player rather than floating in front of the lens.
;
; IT IS THE LEFT HAND, AND IT IS A MIRROR — NOT A MOVE (user ruling).
;
; Why the left: every sword style idles its grip at camera-space x = +0.30
; (`moveset_library.gd`), so a chain grip on the right shares the sword's quadrant and the
; weapon switch reads as a swap in place rather than as a different hand.
;
; Why the basis is mirrored and NOT just the origin moved. The stow fan is welded to this
; anchor through `_handle` (`chain_visual.gd`: `_coil_root.transform` = `Transform3D(_stow_basis(),
; STOW_ANCHOR)`, and `_build_coils` parents it to `_handle`), so the surplus hangs a fixed
; 1.04m to the hand's left and the right→left run IS the fan's lead-in. MEASURED, moving the
; origin alone and re-deriving `STOW_ANCHOR.x` to hold the fan's camera-space place: the run
; halves 1.05m → 0.50m and the path's tightest turn goes 0.1268 → 0.0842 m, under the chain's
; own 0.1110m bend limit, with adjacent links interpenetrating (+0.0023 → −0.0051). The run's
; length is the bend, and it cannot be tuned back (`ARC_LEAD_SLACK`'s window is already
; measured and narrow).
;
; Mirroring the BASIS instead moves the whole rig as one rigid mirror: the hand goes left AND
; the surplus goes to the right hip, the run stays 1.05m, and every reading is preserved
; because a mirror is an isometry. It needs no constant flips at all — including no change to
; `STOW_ANCHOR` — because the fan is expressed in THIS node's space.
;
; A NEGATIVE X SCALE WAS TRIED FIRST AND IS NOT USABLE, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING: mirroring
; this node's BASIS does reproduce every `stow_diag` reading exactly (it is an isometry), and
; it is safe for winding because the chain's material is `CULL_DISABLED` — but Godot refuses a
; determinant-negative basis in `Basis.get_quaternion()`, so every node that reads `.rotation`
; or `.global_rotation` underneath screams `must be normalized in order to be casted to a
; Quaternion`: ~400 errors per run, and STEP 0 forbids a debugger error.
;
; So the mirror is done in `chain_visual.gd` instead, by flipping the stow's own constants
; (see `STOW_ANCHOR` / `ARC_START` / `ARC_SPIN`), which keeps every basis PROPER.
[node name="ChainHandAnchor" type="Node3D" parent="CameraRig/LookPivot/MotionPivot/ShakePivot/WeaponRoot"]
position = Vector3(-0.3, -0.34, -0.62)
